# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The \"optimal\" number of Grover iterations is the number that makes the probability of measuring the marked state as high as possible in the absence of noise. But fewer iterations than that might still make the marked state substantially more likely than other states. So you might be able to get away with fewer iterations than the optimal number. This reduces circuit depth, and thus reduces error rates.\n"
msgstr "Ang \"pinakamainam\" na bilang ng Grover iterations ay ang bilang na nagpapataas ng posibilidad ng pagsusukat ng markadong estado nang pinakamaataas nang wala sa noise. Ngunit ang mas kaunting pag-uulit kaysa roon ay maaari pa ring gawing mas malamang ang markadong estado kaysa sa iba pang mga estado. Kaya, maaari kang makarating sa mas kaunting pag-uulit kaysa sa pinakamainam na bilang. Binabawasan nito ang lalim ng circuit, at sa gayon ay binabawasan ang mga rate ng error.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ##
#, no-wrap
msgid "Activity 3: Solve a Minesweeper grid with Grover's algorithm {#activity-3-solve-a-minesweeper-grid-with-grovers-algorithm}"
msgstr "Aktibidad 3: Lutasin ang isang Minesweeper grid gamit ang Grover's algorithm {#activity-3-solve-a-minesweeper-grid-with-grovers-algorithm}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the previous section, we noted that Grover's algorithm becomes genuinely useful when we can build an oracle from the *constraints* of a problem, rather than from knowledge of the answer. Minesweeper is a perfect example: the numbered cells tell us how many mines are adjacent, and those constraints fully determine where the mines must be &mdash; but finding the configuration requires search.\n"
msgstr "Sa nakaraang seksyon, napansin natin na ang Grover's algorithm ay tunay na nagiging kapaki-pakinabang kapag maaari tayong bumuo ng oracle mula sa mga *constraint* ng isang problema, sa halip na mula sa kaalaman ng sagot. Ang Minesweeper ay isang perpektong halimbawa: ang mga numeradong cell ay nagsasabi sa atin kung ilang mina ang katabing, at ang mga constraint na iyon ay ganap na nagtatakda kung nasaan dapat ang mga mina &mdash; ngunit ang paghanap ng configuration ay nangangailangan ng paghahanap.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Minesweeper has been proven to be NP-complete: it is hard to solve but easy to check. That makes it a natural candidate for Grover's algorithm. Of course, we cannot yet solve a full 9$\\times$9 grid on a noisy quantum computer &mdash; the circuits would be far too deep. Instead, we will use a tiny grid as a toy demonstration of how one would approach a larger board on a future fault-tolerant machine.\n"
msgstr "Napatunayan na ang Minesweeper ay NP-complete: mahirap lutasin ngunit madaling suriin. Ginagawa itong natural na kandidato para sa Grover's algorithm. Siyempre, hindi pa tayo makakalutas ng buong 9$\\times$9 grid sa isang maingay na quantum computer &mdash; ang mga circuit ay magiging masyadong malalim. Sa halip, gagamit tayo ng napakaliit na grid bilang isang laruan na demonstrasyon kung paano lalalapitin ang isang mas malaking board sa isang hinaharap na fault-tolerant na makina.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A few important caveats. Grover's algorithm provides only a quadratic speedup over *unstructured* classical search. Minesweeper almost certainly has exploitable structure that a clever classical algorithm could use. And for an exponentially growing search space, even the $\\sqrt{N}$ improvement only goes so far. But let us set those concerns aside and use this toy problem to illustrate how problem constraints get encoded into a quantum oracle.\n"
msgstr "Ilang mahahalagang caveat. Ang Grover's algorithm ay nagbibigay lamang ng quadratic speedup kumpara sa *unstructured* na klasikal na paghahanap. Ang Minesweeper ay halos tiyak na may magagamit na istraktura na maaaring gamitin ng isang matalinong klasikal na algorithm. At para sa exponentially lumalagong espasyo ng paghahanap, kahit ang $\\sqrt{N}$ na pagpapabuti ay may limitasyon. Ngunit itabi natin muna ang mga alalahanin na iyon at gamitin ang laruang problemang ito para ilarawan kung paano ang mga constraint ng problema ay na-encode sa isang quantum oracle.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "The grid {#the-grid}"
msgstr "Ang grid {#the-grid}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Here is our baby minesweeper grid:\n"
msgstr "Narito ang ating baby minesweeper grid:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![A simple Minesweeper grid with three blank cells and three numbered cells.](/learning/images/modules/computer-science/grovers/minesweeper-grid.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Isang simpleng Minesweeper grid na may tatlong blangkong cell at tatlong numeradong cell.](/learning/images/modules/computer-science/grovers/minesweeper-grid.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each blank cell can be represented by a binary variable indicating whether it contains a mine. We label these $x_0$, $x_1$, and $x_2$, where $x_i = 1$ means there is a mine on that cell and $x_i = 0$ means there is not:\n"
msgstr "Ang bawat blangkong cell ay maaaring katawanin ng isang binary na variable na nagpapahiwatig kung naglalaman ito ng isang mina. Lalagyan natin ito ng label na $x_0$, $x_1$, at $x_2$, kung saan ang $x_i = 1$ ay nangangahulugang may mina sa cell na iyon at ang $x_i = 0$ ay nangangahulugang wala:\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![The same Minesweeper grid with variables x0, x1, x2 labeling the blank cells.](/learning/images/modules/computer-science/grovers/minesweeper-grid-labeled.avif)\n"
msgstr "![Ang parehong Minesweeper grid na may mga variable na x0, x1, x2 na nag-etiketsa sa mga blangkong cell.](/learning/images/modules/computer-science/grovers/minesweeper-grid-labeled.avif)\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We could solve this in our heads in about half a second, but we are using this toy problem to illustrate how a much harder board could be approached with a quantum computer.\n"
msgstr "Maaari nating lutasin ito sa ating isipan sa halos kalahating segundo, ngunit ginagamit natin ang laruang problemang ito para ilarawan kung paano malalapitan ang isang mas mahirap na board gamit ang isang quantum computer.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Encode the constraints {#encode-the-constraints}"
msgstr "I-encode ang mga constraint {#encode-the-constraints}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Each numbered cell places a condition on the adjacent blank cells. We need to express these conditions as Boolean expressions that can be encoded into a quantum circuit.\n"
msgstr "Ang bawat numeradong cell ay naglalagay ng kondisyon sa mga katabing blangkong cell. Kailangan nating ipahayag ang mga kondisyon na ito bilang mga Boolean na ekspresyon na maaaring i-encode sa isang quantum circuit.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The \"1\" cell adjacent to $x_0$ and $x_1$ says that exactly one of them contains a mine. This is precisely the exclusive-OR (XOR) operation, $\\oplus$, which returns true when exactly one of its inputs is true:\n"
"$$\n"
"(x_0 \\oplus x_1)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang cell na \"1\" na katabi ng $x_0$ at $x_1$ ay nagsasabi na eksaktong isa sa kanila ang naglalaman ng mina. Ito ay eksakto ang exclusive-OR (XOR) na operasyon, $\\oplus$, na nagbabalik ng true kapag eksaktong isa sa mga input nito ay true:\n"
"$$\n"
"(x_0 \\oplus x_1)\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Similarly, the other \"1\" cell (adjacent to $x_1$ and $x_2$) gives us:\n"
"$$\n"
"(x_1 \\oplus x_2)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"Katulad nito, ang isa pang cell na \"1\" (katabi ng $x_1$ at $x_2$) ay nagbibigay sa atin ng:\n"
"$$\n"
"(x_1 \\oplus x_2)\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The \"2\" cell says that two of the three blank cells must contain mines. Since XOR is a parity operation, $x_0 \\oplus x_1 \\oplus x_2$ returns true when an *odd* number of the variables are true. We want an *even* number (specifically two) to be true, so we negate with $\\lnot$:\n"
"$$\n"
"\\lnot(x_0 \\oplus x_1 \\oplus x_2)\n"
"$$\n"
"On its own, this expression would be satisfied by either zero or two qubits in the $|1\\rangle$ state, since it is a statement about parity. But combined with the other two clauses, which each require at least one mine, the only satisfying assignment has exactly two mines.\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang cell na \"2\" ay nagsasabi na dalawa sa tatlong blangkong cell ang dapat maglaman ng mga mina. Dahil ang XOR ay isang parity na operasyon, ang $x_0 \\oplus x_1 \\oplus x_2$ ay nagbabalik ng true kapag *gansal* na bilang ng mga variable ay true. Gusto namin ng *pares* na bilang (partikular na dalawa) na maging true, kaya itinatanggi namin gamit ang $\\lnot$:\n"
"$$\n"
"\\lnot(x_0 \\oplus x_1 \\oplus x_2)\n"
"$$\n"
"Nang mag-isa, ang ekspresyong ito ay matutugunan ng alinman sa zero o dalawang qubit sa $|1\\rangle$ na estado, dahil ito ay isang pahayag tungkol sa parity. Ngunit kasama ang iba pang dalawang clause, na bawat isa ay nangangailangan ng hindi bababa sa isang mina, ang tanging nakakatugon na assignment ay may eksaktong dalawang mina.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"All three conditions must be simultaneously satisfied, so we join them with and symbols $\\land$:\n"
"$$\n"
"(x_0 \\oplus x_1) \\;\\land\\; (x_1 \\oplus x_2) \\;\\land\\; \\lnot(x_0 \\oplus x_1 \\oplus x_2)\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""
"Ang tatlong kondisyon ay dapat matugunan nang sabay-sabay, kaya pinagsama namin sila gamit ang mga simbolo ng and $\\land$:\n"
"$$\n"
"(x_0 \\oplus x_1) \\;\\land\\; (x_1 \\oplus x_2) \\;\\land\\; \\lnot(x_0 \\oplus x_1 \\oplus x_2)\n"
"$$\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Step 1: Map classical inputs to a quantum problem {#step-1-map-classical-inputs-to-a-quantum-problem-2}"
msgstr "Hakbang 1: I-map ang mga klasikal na input sa isang quantum na problema {#step-1-map-classical-inputs-to-a-quantum-problem-2}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we need to encode this Boolean expression into a quantum circuit that serves as the oracle. The quantum version of XOR can be accomplished with CX (CNOT) gates: applying two CX gates from the data qubits to a workspace (ancilla) qubit effectively computes their XOR and stores the result in the ancilla.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon kailangan nating i-encode ang Boolean na ekspresyong ito sa isang quantum circuit na nagsisilbing oracle. Ang quantum na bersyon ng XOR ay maaaring gawin gamit ang mga CX (CNOT) gate: ang pag-apply ng dalawang CX gate mula sa mga data qubit patungo sa isang workspace (ancilla) qubit ay epektibong nagkukuwenta ng kanilang XOR at nag-iimbak ng resulta sa ancilla.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "We introduce three workspace qubits &mdash; one for each clause. We store the result of each Boolean expression in its corresponding workspace qubit, then use a multi-controlled Z gate to flip the phase of the three-qubit state that makes all three workspace qubits $|1\\rangle$ (meaning all clauses are satisfied simultaneously).\n"
msgstr "Nagpapakilala tayo ng tatlong workspace qubit &mdash; isa para sa bawat clause. Iniimbak natin ang resulta ng bawat Boolean na ekspresyon sa katumbas na workspace qubit nito, pagkatapos ay gumagamit ng multi-controlled Z gate para i-flip ang phase ng three-qubit state na gumagawa ng lahat ng tatlong workspace qubit na $|1\\rangle$ (ibig sabihin ang lahat ng clause ay natutugunan nang sabay-sabay).\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "In the first code cell below, we build the \"compute\" half of the oracle &mdash; the part that evaluates each clause and writes the result into the workspace qubits.\n"
msgstr "Sa unang code cell sa ibaba, binubuo natin ang \"compute\" na kalahati ng oracle &mdash; ang bahaging nagse-evaluate ng bawat clause at nagsusulat ng resulta sa mga workspace qubit.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "At this point, the result of each clause is stored in its corresponding workspace qubit. Now we need the three-qubit data state that makes all three workspace qubits $|1\\rangle$ to pick up a minus sign. We do this with a multi-controlled Z gate (implemented as an MCX gate sandwiched by Hadamard gates on the target).\n"
msgstr "Sa puntong ito, ang resulta ng bawat clause ay nakaimbak sa katumbas na workspace qubit nito. Ngayon kailangan ang three-qubit data state na gumagawa ng lahat ng tatlong workspace qubit na $|1\\rangle$ para kumuha ng minus sign. Ginagawa natin ito gamit ang isang multi-controlled Z gate (ipinatupad bilang isang MCX gate na napapalibutan ng mga Hadamard gate sa target).\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "After applying the phase flip, we must **uncompute** &mdash; undo all the clause-evaluation steps in reverse order &mdash; to reset the workspace qubits back to $|0\\rangle$. This is essential so that the workspace qubits are clean for subsequent iterations of the Grover operator.\n"
msgstr "Pagkatapos i-apply ang phase flip, kailangan nating **i-uncompute** &mdash; i-undo ang lahat ng mga hakbang ng clause-evaluation sa kabaligtarang pagkakasunod-sunod &mdash; para i-reset ang mga workspace qubit pabalik sa $|0\\rangle$. Mahalaga ito upang ang mga workspace qubit ay malinis para sa mga kasunod na pag-uulit ng Grover operator.\n"

# doq: mechanical transfer from the previous translation
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "This circuit is our oracle: it flips the phase of the data-qubit state that satisfies all three Minesweeper constraints, and leaves the workspace qubits back in $|0\\rangle$.\n"
msgstr "Ang circuit na ito ang ating oracle: nagba-flip ito ng phase ng data-qubit state na nakakatugon sa lahat ng tatlong Minesweeper constraint, at iniiwan ang mga workspace qubit pabalik sa $|0\\rangle$.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we construct the full Grover operator from this oracle. Note the `reflection_qubits` argument: we pass only the data qubits `x`, because the workspace qubits are not part of the search space. Their job is done once the oracle has been applied.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon bumubuo tayo ng buong Grover operator mula sa oracle na ito. Pansinin ang `reflection_qubits` argument: nagpapasa lang tayo ng mga data qubit `x`, dahil ang mga workspace qubit ay hindi bahagi ng espasyo ng paghahanap. Tapos na ang kanilang trabaho kapag nailapat na ang oracle.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "With three data qubits and one solution state, the optimal number of Grover iterations is $t \\approx \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\sqrt{8} - \\frac{1}{2} \\approx 1.7$, so we use two iterations. We apply Hadamard gates to the data qubits to create the initial superposition, compose the Grover operator twice, and measure only the data qubits.\n"
msgstr "Sa tatlong data qubit at isang estado ng solusyon, ang pinakamainam na bilang ng Grover iteration ay $t \\approx \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\sqrt{8} - \\frac{1}{2} \\approx 1.7$, kaya gumagamit tayo ng dalawang pag-uulit. Nag-a-apply tayo ng mga Hadamard gate sa mga data qubit para lumikha ng paunang superposition, kino-compose ang Grover operator nang dalawang beses, at sinusukat lamang ang mga data qubit.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Step 2: Optimize problem for quantum hardware execution {#step-2-optimize-problem-for-quantum-hardware-execution-2}"
msgstr "Hakbang 2: I-optimize ang problema para sa pagpapatakbo sa quantum hardware {#step-2-optimize-problem-for-quantum-hardware-execution-2}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "As before, we transpile the circuit for the target backend.\n"
msgstr "Tulad ng dati, tina-transpile natin ang circuit para sa target backend.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Now we can check the depth of the transpiled circuit. Because the Minesweeper oracle uses workspace qubits and multiple CX gates, the transpiled circuit will be deeper than those in the previous activities.\n"
msgstr "Ngayon maaari na nating suriin ang lalim ng transpiled na circuit. Dahil ang Minesweeper oracle ay gumagamit ng mga workspace qubit at maraming CX gate, ang transpiled na circuit ay magiging mas malalim kaysa sa mga mula sa mga nakaraang aktibidad.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, fuzzy, no-wrap
#| msgid "Step 3: Execute using Qiskit primitives {#step-3-execute-using-qiskit-primitives-2}"
msgid "Step 3: Execute using IBM Quantum primitives {#step-3-execute-using-ibm-quantum-primitives-2}"
msgstr "Hakbang 3: I-execute gamit ang Qiskit primitives {#step-3-execute-using-qiskit-primitives-2}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Title ###
#, no-wrap
msgid "Step 4: Post-process and return result in desired classical format {#step-4-post-process-and-return-result-in-desired-classical-format-2}"
msgstr "Hakbang 4: I-post-process at ibalik ang resulta sa nais na klasikal na format {#step-4-post-process-and-return-result-in-desired-classical-format-2}"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "The `101` state should appear with far higher probability than any other, indicating that mines are located at $x_0$ and $x_2$. We have used a quantum computer to solve a tiny game of Minesweeper!\n"
msgstr "Ang estado na `101` ay dapat lumabas nang may mas mataas na posibilidad kaysa sa anumang iba pa, na nagpapahiwatig na ang mga mina ay matatagpuan sa $x_0$ at $x_2$. Ginamit natin ang isang quantum computer para lutasin ang isang maliit na laro ng Minesweeper!\n"

# doq-bootstrap: positional
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Of course, the best classical algorithms for minesweeper are better than a brute-force search through all possible mine configurations &mdash; they exploit the structure of the grid. Grover's algorithm would only offer an advantage on extremely difficult boards designed to be maximally ambiguous, and even then, the quadratic speedup means it cannot keep pace with exponential growth indefinitely. But the real takeaway is the technique: encoding problem constraints into a quantum oracle is a powerful pattern that extends to constraint satisfaction, combinatorial optimization, and many other domains.\n"
msgstr "Siyempre, ang pinakamahusay na mga klasikal na algorithm para sa minesweeper ay mas mahusay kaysa sa isang brute-force na paghahanap sa lahat ng posibleng configuration ng mina &mdash; pinagsasamantalahan nila ang istraktura ng grid. Ang Grover's algorithm ay mag-aalok lamang ng kalamangan sa mga napakahirap na board na dinisenyo para maging pinaka-malabo, at kahit noon, ang quadratic speedup ay hindi makakasabay sa exponential na paglago nang walang katiyakan. Ngunit ang tunay na aral ay ang pamamaraan: ang pag-encode ng mga constraint ng problema sa isang quantum oracle ay isang makapangyarihang pattern na umaabot sa constraint satisfaction, combinatorial optimization, at marami pang ibang domain.\n"

